Original commit body from 2d4efaed69c6d1868cc109087ad4f76779c3de5a:

go.yaml shipped 20 method rows for gin, echo, fiber and gRPC. Two independent
reasons to remove them, either sufficient alone.

THEY ARE THIRD-PARTY, AND THE ADR ALREADY DECIDED THIS. ADR-0016 scopes the
built-in catalogue to the stdlib -- "a curated list of stdlib functions, not an
unbounded set of library APIs" (sec 27) -- and sec 35 resolves exactly this gap
as "a project-local overlay, not more built-in rows", with sec 55 placing worked
examples under docs/ because "the user owns those rows".

AND THEY MATCHED NOTHING. They declared a package-IDENTIFIER module
(`gin.Context`) while the Go analyzer emits the module PATH it read from the
import (`github.com/gin-gonic/gin`). Those never compare equal:

    lookup_with_module("JSON", "github.com/gin-gonic/gin", cc="method") -> None
    lookup_with_module("JSON", "gin",                      cc="method")
        -> gin.Context.JSON

The second line is what a catalogue-reach probe produces when its fixture writes
`import ("gin")` -- an import no Go program contains. That is how twenty rows
that could never fire kept scoring as reachable.

SO THIS IS A MOVE PLUS A CORRECTION, NOT A DELETION.
docs/io-primitives-overlays/go-web-frameworks.yaml re-declares all twenty against
the real import path, including the /vN suffix that Go's semantic import
versioning makes part of the path (`github.com/labstack/echo/v4.Context`). It
pairs with INV-javid in the same release: before that fix a /vN import bound the
alias `vN`, so echo and fiber would have stayed unreachable however the catalogue
spelled them.

MEASURED on an idiomatic Go fixture, both arms, with an os.WriteFile control
firing in each so a zero is distinguishable from a fixture that resolves nothing:

    shipped catalogue alone (post-cull)   0 framework chains
    + go-web-frameworks.yaml              6  gin Context.JSON / Engine.Run,
                                             echo Context.JSON / Echo.Start,
                                             fiber App.Listen, grpc Server.Serve

All six attribute to the calling function. Before this change the same fixture
produced zero in BOTH arms.

NON-DESTRUCTION ASSERTED, NOT ASSUMED, because when "no change" is the correct
answer a broken run looks better than a working one. A/B on real Go repos that
import these frameworks, re-adding the pre-cull spellings through the overlay
channel so the second arm reproduces the old built-in behaviour exactly:

    keda      (173 framework-importing files)  105 edges / 145 chains both arms
    swarmkit   (51 framework-importing files)  122 edges / 133 chains both arms
    0 chains lost, 0 gained, on either.

NOT MEASURED: distribution (85 framework-importing files) did not complete within
its window -- one arm never wrote output. Two repos, not three.

THE CONTRACT IS PINNED FROM BOTH ENDS, IN BOTH PACKAGES, because the defect WAS
the two ends drifting apart unnoticed for a full measurement cycle:
  - test_go_web_framework_overlay (core) asserts each row resolves from the
    literal import path, AND -- the negative half -- that the old short hint
    (gin, echo, fiber, grpc) now returns None, so the two spellings cannot
    quietly coexist and keep a fabricated-import probe calling these reachable;
  - TestFrameworkImportPathsReachTheModuleSlot (mainstream) asserts the analyzer
    emits those same literal strings into the module slot.
CI tests packages in isolation, so neither can check the other's half. Asserting
identical literals from opposite ends is what makes a one-sided edit fail.

STILL SHIPPING IN go.yaml AND DELIBERATELY NOT TOUCHED: 33 further third-party
rows (golang.org/x/sys/unix 27, google.golang.org/grpc functions 3,
golang.org/x/sys/execabs 3). Unlike the 20 these are REACHABLE -- _module_matches
compares trailing components and `unix` IS the trailing component of
golang.org/x/sys/unix -- so culling them would delete working detections rather
than dead rows. That is a real trade-off between ADR-0016 scope compliance and
default detection, and it is filed for a decision rather than taken here.

TWO EXISTING TESTS WERE ENCODING THE DEFECT AND ARE CORRECTED, NOT DELETED.
smart-test selected only the two new files for this change and went green, which
was a FALSE GREEN -- go.yaml is a shipped data file whose consumers it does not
map. Running the io-boundary suite directly found both:

  test_go_catalog_tls_smtp          asserted lookup("Data", "gin") MATCHES
  test_go_qualified_run_still_matches  asserted go:gin.Engine:...:Run MATCHES

Both were asserting against a hint no Go program can emit, so each was testing
the fixture rather than the gate. Each now makes the same point against a stdlib
row the analyzer really does produce (net/smtp.Client.Data, os/exec.Cmd.Run), and
the comment records why the row moved. The neighbouring docstrings and go.yaml's
`ambiguous_names` comments named gin/echo/grpc rows that no longer ship; those
are corrected too, with the framework names kept in `ambiguous_names` because
they still need guarding once the overlay IS loaded.

Also worth recording: the original comment on the Data case read "gin.Context
.Data vs template.Data". There is no `template.Data` row in go.yaml and there
never was -- the real collision is with net/smtp.Client.Data. The comment had
been wrong independently of this change.

